#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555804 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100807 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dcb621ad-bcc5-57ea-9274-d2bbe93df2ce" :knowledge-point/title "Expanding a Binomial With More Than One Variable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39552913-1c07-56b5-b6b4-c97f61c68646" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{6}}{{y}^{3}}-3{x}^{4}z+3{x}^{2}{z}^{2}{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555123} {:answer/id #uuid "4e9eedfb-df31-5fef-89f8-4a0a5bc39de6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{6}}{{y}^{3}}-\\frac{4{x}^{4}z}{y}+6{x}^{2}{z}^{2}{y}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555177} {:answer/id #uuid "738b92d9-e5fc-511d-904f-c37c0cfe4696" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{6}}{{y}^{3}}+\\frac{3{x}^{2}z}{y}+3x{z}^{2}{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555259} {:answer/id #uuid "a1e6de29-d31f-58b5-b882-4e6e62b0a4a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{6}}{{y}^{3}}-\\frac{3{x}^{4}z}{y}+3{x}^{2}{z}^{2}{y}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555389} {:answer/id #uuid "e5810984-6602-54b8-9000-97b31b9afc00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{6}}{{y}^{3}}-4{x}^{4}z+4{x}^{2}{z}^{2}{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555563}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "39552913-1c07-56b5-b6b4-c97f61c68646" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{6}}{{y}^{3}}-3{x}^{4}z+3{x}^{2}{z}^{2}{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555123} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d1ececc-599e-58e1-a6af-f6ea1d9aa196" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555659}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5386af7a-f43c-5f81-bd4e-2f8f31eb31ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-110680" :question/problem "Find, in ascending powers of $z$ and descending powers of $x,$ the first three terms of the binomial expansion of ${(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}-z{y}^{2})}^{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's compare our expression with the binomial\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}.\n$$\n\nHere we have that $n=3,$ $a=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y},$ and $b=-z{y}^{2}.$ So, the exponents of the binomial expansion must follow the pattern\n\n$$\n{a}^{3}{b}^{0},\\,{a}^{2}{b}^{1},\\,{a}^{1}{b}^{2},\\,{a}^{0}{b}^{3}.\n$$\n\nThe $3$rd row in Pascal's Triangle is $(1,3,3,1).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}-z{y}^{2})}^{3} & =1{a}^{3}{b}^{0}+3{a}^{2}b+3a{b}^{2}+1{a}^{0}{b}^{3} \\\\  & =1{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y})}^{3}{(-z{y}^{2})}^{0}+3{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y})}^{2}{(-z{y}^{2})}^{1} \\\\  & \\,+3{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y})}^{1}{(-z{y}^{2})}^{2}+1{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y})}^{0}{(-z{y}^{2})}^{3} \\\\  & =1(\\frac{{x}^{6}}{{y}^{3}})(1)+3(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{y}^{2}})(-z{y}^{2}) \\\\  & \\,+3(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y})({z}^{2}{y}^{4})+1(1)(-{z}^{3}{y}^{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{6}}{{y}^{3}}-\\frac{3{x}^{4}z{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}}+\\frac{3{x}^{2}{z}^{2}{y}^{4}}{y}-{z}^{3}{y}^{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{6}}{{y}^{3}}-3{x}^{4}z+3{x}^{2}{z}^{2}{y}^{3}-{z}^{3}{y}^{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind, in ascending powers of $z$ and descending powers of $x,$ the first three terms are\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{6}}{{y}^{3}}-3{x}^{4}z+3{x}^{2}{z}^{2}{y}^{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555821 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192634 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5bc2c576-4f7c-5b4a-8c78-8a41420fdcce" :knowledge-point/title "Isolating an Absolute Value Expression"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "77043ef8-d223-5af1-b0ce-b725c2b1b3f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555275}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77043ef8-d223-5af1-b0ce-b725c2b1b3f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555275} :answer-field/id #uuid "06e19274-3bee-5fd0-8c5c-ee3a6c3f2176" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555642}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "72df5984-46fa-528d-8ad1-e8f21e9e20f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-212479" :question/problem "Consider the absolute value equation\n\n$$\n3|x+1|-4=8.\n$$\n\nThis equation can be expressed in the following equivalent form:\n\n$|x+1|=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We can rearrange the equation to isolate the absolute value on the left side:\n\nFirst, we add $4$ to both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3|x+1|-4 & =8 \\\\ 3|x+1|-4+4 & =8+4 \\\\ 3|x+1| & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide both sides of the equation by $3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3|x+1| & =12 \\\\ \\frac{3|x+1|}{3} & =\\frac{12}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{3}|x+1|}{\\cancel{3}} & =4 \\\\ |x+1| & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555836 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105706 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "649ee093-c7ba-5832-9ffb-6d39a351573b" :knowledge-point/title "Fractions With Radicals in the Numerator and Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a214e20-11ee-5746-8810-076a062c13ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555160} {:answer/id #uuid "5573251c-6bf9-557c-a86c-d0de8177c93e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555192} {:answer/id #uuid "7e6d0021-df51-5890-84d9-4c364481d3fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555291} {:answer/id #uuid "813abaaf-ebbb-5bad-8723-5b8696d33fab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{42}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555297} {:answer/id #uuid "fb149d86-bb17-5026-a277-cf3474adfb64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555620}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb149d86-bb17-5026-a277-cf3474adfb64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555620} :answer-field/id #uuid "00b9f5df-6016-5b3f-a644-4963b4ea3a42" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555638}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "97180f1c-2846-5a93-ae87-17ba0602d531" :question/math-academy-id "q-212910" :question/problem "$\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3\\sqrt{7}}=$" :question/worked-solution "To remove the radical $\\sqrt{7}$ from the denominator, all we need to do is multiply both the numerator and denominator by $\\sqrt{7}$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3\\sqrt{7}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3\\sqrt{7}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{\\sqrt{7}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{7}}{3\\cdot (\\sqrt{7})^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{7}}{3\\cdot 7} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{7}}{21} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the product rule to simplify the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{7}}{21} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2\\cdot 7}}{21} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{21} & \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555823 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068679 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "42cc9880-912e-5dce-9b93-e6af88a411c4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Length of the Hypotenuse Given the Length of the Legs of a Right Triangle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0664bfa8-2d01-5d3d-ad93-e8fca7955080" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}\\,cm" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555010} {:answer/id #uuid "31089567-fd42-54a4-a8d0-48cfa5c4e330" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{5}\\,cm" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555105} {:answer/id #uuid "636d0263-650d-56a4-9b10-6dc939b66500" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}\\,cm" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555221} {:answer/id #uuid "7d8e0d22-8c98-5dcf-9e27-c996945628ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,cm" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555288} {:answer/id #uuid "e9a534c8-2d50-5c60-9824-ae02704c4eca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,cm" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555573}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "636d0263-650d-56a4-9b10-6dc939b66500" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}\\,cm" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555221} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f1768e5-8700-506e-98db-0bb9abb61815" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555693}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "763919e9-e5c7-5fb3-9ee3-feea81f9fd20" :question/math-academy-id "q-22902" :question/problem "A right triangle has legs of length $2\\,\\text{cm}$ and $4\\,\\text{cm}.$ Calculate the length of its hypotenuse." :question/worked-solution "Let $a=2\\,cm,$ $b=4\\,cm,$ and $c$ be the length of the hypotenuse. According to the Pythagorean theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}^{2} & ={a}^{2}+{b}^{2} \\\\ c & =\\sqrt{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in the values for $a$ and $b$ gives us\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\sqrt{(2)^{2}+(4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{20} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}\\,cm.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555761 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110720 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6cd2f04-e0d5-5df0-8e74-ea737501cdf8" :knowledge-point/title "Solving For an Unknown Parameter"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b03681d2-3f8d-5d5a-b665-08eb5334df77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555422}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b03681d2-3f8d-5d5a-b665-08eb5334df77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555422} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8685c5a-c976-5c4d-ba32-65fdf5654aca" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555734}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "06a0435e-893d-5476-8004-8e6faf0aa039" :question/math-academy-id "q-288569" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(4r-6)x=22x\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is a constant. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $r?$\n\n$r=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=bx\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $a=b,$ then the equation has infinitely many solutions.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax=bx.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na=4r-6,\\,b=22.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\n4r-6=22.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $r,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4r & =28 \\\\ r & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555859 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054802 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3986a3cd-c543-54e9-8d55-f744d9436313" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Line Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "347c2f0c-5a52-5eed-a5f6-845a1b198245" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555111} {:answer/id #uuid "48db9c66-7f10-5f23-8a17-8771c3a07517" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555157} {:answer/id #uuid "95cbbbb8-182c-54d0-b493-2f62f281a0dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555357} {:answer/id #uuid "db217f00-30c9-50d9-a7c1-29d4b1d834be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555531} {:answer/id #uuid "dbc989c2-ca66-5d8e-9dc2-695894977ce3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555533}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "48db9c66-7f10-5f23-8a17-8771c3a07517" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555157} :answer-field/id #uuid "6cfeab61-6e76-5a26-9da7-d3d287d0e865" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555692}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d96a4476-f395-552e-94ef-9cd8de4e9f95" :question/math-academy-id "q-54292" :question/problem "![](images/ac/ac28d44b12c5804a36cc9ae7a3655237449bfe5f1933df81d70ece5ee18ed981.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the graph of the straight line above?\n\nI. $y=-4x+2$\n\nII. $y=x+4$\n\nIII. $y=-2x+4$" :question/worked-solution "Let's pick two distinct points on the line. For example, $A$ and $B$ with coordinates $(0,4)$ and $(2,0)$, respectively.\n\n![](images/47/4719dc10bbf4078e6b266a36b50a82e117b3e775395a8a3e44c0dff4ae56f15b.png)\n\nNow, we substitute the coordinates of both points in our equations. If both points satisfy an equation, then we can conclude that the equation represents the line in the graph. Otherwise, if any point does *not* satisfy the equation, then we can conclude that the equation does *not* represent the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation I: $y=-4x+2.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,4)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4) & =-4(0)+2 \\\\ 4 & =2\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=-4x+2$ is *not* equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation II: $y=x+4.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,4)$ and $B(2,0)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4) & =(0)+4 \\\\ 4 & =4\\,✓ \\\\ (0) & =(2)+4 \\\\ 0 & =6\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=x+4$ is *not* equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation III: $y=-2x+4.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,4)$ and $B(2,0)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4) & =-2(0)+4 \\\\ 4 & =4\\,✓ \\\\ (0) & =-2(2)+4 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=-2x+4$ is an equation of the line in the graph.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equation that represented the line in the graph was $y=-2x+4,$ corresponding to answer choice III. Therefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555869 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073887 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f78d8f50-ae86-59e6-8c88-abc42a6a9fc2" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Whole Number Unit Rates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0777c429-4126-5227-8671-6a34e94d7cb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Isabella washed $3$ windows per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555012} {:answer/id #uuid "58842088-1320-504a-b699-b86637aaf14a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Isabella washed $\\frac{1}{3}$ of a window per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555194} {:answer/id #uuid "bf4ceed0-6052-5111-b5ba-045d89c07a73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Isabella washed $6$ windows per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555458} {:answer/id #uuid "c896cb4d-9d0d-516c-ac40-a9ffe1766b52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Isabella washed $\\frac{1}{6}$ of a window per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555481} {:answer/id #uuid "cbde90ad-dcd7-5a36-ad24-53913d79dc77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Isabella washed $2$ windows per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555489}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf4ceed0-6052-5111-b5ba-045d89c07a73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Isabella washed $6$ windows per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555458} :answer-field/id #uuid "27412f37-44b6-5f69-9944-94e2934ed7dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555657}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ede2a25e-f545-5338-983c-ea1ddced5b57" :question/math-academy-id "q-60049" :question/problem "Isabella took $2$ hours to wash $12$ windows. If she washes the windows at a steady pace, then what statement describes the unit rate?" :question/worked-solution "The ratio of washed windows to hours is $12:2.$ So we start with the ratio $12:2$ and write it as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{12}{2}\n$$\n\nWe can make a denominator of $1$ by dividing the numerator and denominator by $2$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{12÷2}{2÷2}=\\frac{6}{1}\n$$\n\nConsequently, Isabella washed $6$ windows per hour."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555775 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073878 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "25e57b78-5159-51bc-aeaf-9530f641262b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Converse of the Perpendicular Bisector Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fa365b4-e923-5ee7-948c-0f7e4b18900f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555059} {:answer/id #uuid "335ced4a-c213-5d68-a762-44d841c873a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555109} {:answer/id #uuid "5aa97ed3-0a0c-50fd-8572-26ade33ad99b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555199} {:answer/id #uuid "c8cdeab7-dd91-5581-b022-bc74bf4ee7c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555483} {:answer/id #uuid "c92d8c81-6d47-5ef1-a7b6-44a2a484001c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555485}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1fa365b4-e923-5ee7-948c-0f7e4b18900f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555059} :answer-field/id #uuid "436bedcf-5ab9-57ff-b69c-7f7c75355960" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555672}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "29fce570-bbfd-5809-b558-27c71c49ba05" :question/math-academy-id "q-67769" :question/problem "![](images/16/16e8f82922ebd5d4b10729e2935c893dd59a7dfb8faa69dde4567c7036209d7d.png)\n\nSolve for $x$ in the figure above given that $AC=BC$ and $\\overset{⟷}{CD}⊥\\overset{―}{AB}.$" :question/worked-solution "By the converse of the perpendicular bisector theorem, a point equidistant from the endpoints of a segment lies on the perpendicular bisector of the segment. \n\nHere, the point $C$ is equidistant from the endpoints of $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ Therefore, $\\overset{⟷}{CD}$ is the perpendicular bisector of $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ \n\nIn particular, we have that $DA=DB.$ We can use this equation so solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}DA & =DB \\\\ 3x-3 & =x+1 \\\\ 3x-x & =1+3 \\\\ 2x & =4 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555813 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110759 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9d62f753-a3e0-5c7e-919d-3f878c1d8e93" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Measure of Unknown Angles on a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6fb85e16-c209-5004-9c31-da49374b8fa2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{30}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555254} {:answer/id #uuid "85fae8db-017c-5ed0-a8a5-edacd74b5ee5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{40}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555309} {:answer/id #uuid "8ae0ff7c-743d-565a-adf6-91feb5251b55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{45}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555325} {:answer/id #uuid "debc1515-abd7-599d-ac45-08a3bd014040" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{25}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555540} {:answer/id #uuid "eeccccad-b6d9-5394-b737-bfc9043dc537" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{35}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555587}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6fb85e16-c209-5004-9c31-da49374b8fa2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{30}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555254} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a065792-7ae1-5541-a4f8-b06c6ad6b15b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555674}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5fcf3b89-deb2-57c7-9289-4881c9262fc3" :question/math-academy-id "q-76803" :question/problem "![](images/ff/ff7f82365a0b2253c43ad441a4af93354ae54ae027fbd2390634b64728cfb210.png)\n\nIn the figure shown above, determine the measure of $∠AOB.$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, $∠COF$ is a straight angle. So, the angle addition postulate gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠COD+m∠DOE+m∠EOF & =m∠COF \\\\ {75}^{∘}+m∠DOE+{75}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠DOE & ={30}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also note that $∠AOB$ and $∠DOE$ are vertical angles. Therefore, since vertical angles are congruent, we have\n\n$$\nm∠AOB=m∠DOE={30}^{∘}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555768 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110626 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dcd3e634-d64c-57dc-9d4c-8f7f67c731ab" :knowledge-point/title "Creating an Exponential Growth Expression Using Percentage Growth"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22157acf-db7c-512b-8c26-ddd6528d48c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\cdot (1.8)^{50}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555065} {:answer/id #uuid "34c59582-bd38-5896-aa78-0791b61291ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\cdot (0.8)^{50}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555112} {:answer/id #uuid "4a5f0374-d9de-5c31-9172-a64c1ef6174d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "50\\cdot (1.8)^{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555162} {:answer/id #uuid "be21ce0f-9a04-589d-bf55-9e082e062371" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "50\\cdot (1.08)^{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555454} {:answer/id #uuid "c333b68a-f76f-541c-8467-a1c446d09e3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\cdot (0.08)^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555471}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be21ce0f-9a04-589d-bf55-9e082e062371" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "50\\cdot (1.08)^{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555454} :answer-field/id #uuid "0db388ed-bde6-5229-8b58-1ef5e13f677d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555645}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1b78dc69-6363-5c74-acb7-15e9e0faab04" :question/math-academy-id "q-86553" :question/problem "Mike purchased an $80$'s baseball card for $$50.$ If the value of the card increases by $8%$ each year, what expression gives the card's value after $10$ years?" :question/worked-solution "The card's value is $$50$, and this number increases by $8%$ each year.\n\n$8%$ as a decimal is $0.08.$ Therefore, an increase of $8%$ corresponds to multiplication by a factor of\n\n$$\n1+0.08=1.08.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n- After $1$ year, the value of the card is $50\\cdot (1.08)$ dollars.\n\n- After $2$ years, the value of the card is $50\\cdot (1.08)^{2}$ dollars.\n\n- After $3$ years, the value of the card is $50\\cdot (1.08)^{3}$ dollars.\n\n$\\,⋮$\n\n- After $10$ years, the value of the card is $50\\cdot (1.08)^{10}$ dollars."}]}
